WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.468
What does a FundsXML delivery actually contain?

2
00:00:03.518 --> 00:00:10.211
In this film you get to know the areas of the standard, in business terms, and you look into a real file.

3
00:00:10.261 --> 00:00:12.138
No knowledge of XML is needed.

4
00:00:13.388 --> 00:00:17.775
A FundsXML delivery is like a well-organised folder with fixed sections.

5
00:00:17.825 --> 00:00:21.225
The envelope says who sent it, when, and for which date.

6
00:00:21.275 --> 00:00:27.202
The funds section describes the funds and their share classes, their prices and their portfolios.

7
00:00:27.252 --> 00:00:31.207
The securities section describes every instrument the funds hold.

8
00:00:31.257 --> 00:00:36.815
And further sections carry transactions, documents, national data and regulatory templates.

9
00:00:36.865 --> 00:00:39.649
Each delivery uses only the sections it needs.

10
00:00:40.999 --> 00:00:46.408
One principle runs through the whole standard: static and dynamic data are kept apart.

11
00:00:46.458 --> 00:00:54.088
Static data changes rarely: the name of a fund, its launch date, its legal form, its share classes and their identifiers.

12
00:00:54.138 --> 00:01:01.070
Dynamic data changes with every valuation: the net asset value, the prices, the fund volume and the portfolio.

13
00:01:01.120 --> 00:01:08.877
Receivers load the static data once and update it when it changes, while the dynamic data arrives every day, week or month.

14
00:01:10.227 --> 00:01:12.075
Let us go through the areas.

15
00:01:12.125 --> 00:01:13.633
First, the fund itself.

16
00:01:13.683 --> 00:01:19.326
A FundsXML file can describe a single fund, or an umbrella fund with many sub-funds.

17
00:01:19.376 --> 00:01:24.680
Each fund or sub-fund has share classes: the units that investors actually buy.

18
00:01:24.730 --> 00:01:34.099
Share classes of the same fund can differ in currency, in fees, in the minimum investment, and in whether income is paid out or reinvested.

19
00:01:34.149 --> 00:01:37.149
Each one has its own ISIN, and its own price.

20
00:01:38.499 --> 00:01:40.684
Second, prices and the net asset value.

21
00:01:40.734 --> 00:01:49.911
For every valuation date, the delivery carries the total net assets of the fund, and for each share class the price per unit and the number of units outstanding.

22
00:01:49.961 --> 00:01:59.367
Every amount carries its currency, and an amount can be given in several currencies at once, for example in the fund currency and in the currency of the share class.

23
00:01:59.417 --> 00:02:01.315
So nobody has to convert it again.

24
00:02:02.665 --> 00:02:06.493
Third, the portfolio: what the fund holds on the valuation date.

25
00:02:06.543 --> 00:02:13.401
Each position says how much the fund holds of an instrument, what it is worth, and what weight it has in the fund.

26
00:02:13.451 --> 00:02:22.369
The description of the instrument itself, its name, its issuer, its type, sits in a separate securities section, and the positions only refer to it.

27
00:02:22.419 --> 00:02:27.095
Shares, bonds, fund units, derivatives, deposits and accounts are all covered.

28
00:02:28.445 --> 00:02:32.249
FundsXML relies on common codes instead of free text.

29
00:02:32.299 --> 00:02:35.667
An ISIN identifies a security or a share class.

30
00:02:35.717 --> 00:02:40.758
An LEI identifies a company: the fund, the asset manager or an issuer.

31
00:02:40.808 --> 00:02:44.030
Currencies and countries use their ISO codes.

32
00:02:44.080 --> 00:02:48.612
And fixed lists describe asset types, cost types or fund types.

33
00:02:48.662 --> 00:02:54.357
So every receiver interprets a value in exactly the same way, without guessing.

34
00:02:55.707 --> 00:02:56.782
And there is more.

35
00:02:56.832 --> 00:03:04.344
Transactions record what moved: subscriptions and redemptions of investors, distributions, and income from the portfolio.

36
00:03:04.394 --> 00:03:09.718
Documents link prospectuses, key information documents, factsheets and annual reports.

37
00:03:09.768 --> 00:03:14.468
Country modules add national reporting, for example for Austria and Germany.

38
00:03:14.518 --> 00:03:21.842
And the regulatory section carries the European templates for distributors and insurers; the next film is about those.

39
00:03:22.809 --> 00:03:24.541
Let us look at a real file.

40
00:03:24.591 --> 00:03:30.940
This is one of the realistic FundsXML example files, opened in FreeXmlToolkit, a free XML editor.

41
00:03:30.990 --> 00:03:41.958
It describes a fictional multi-asset fund at the end of October, with made-up but consistent data: hundreds of securities, four share classes, a month of transactions.

42
00:03:42.610 --> 00:03:45.014
Folded, the structure is easy to see.

43
00:03:45.064 --> 00:03:46.938
Control data is the envelope.

44
00:03:46.988 --> 00:03:50.652
Funds holds the fund, with its prices and its portfolio.

45
00:03:50.702 --> 00:03:53.836
Asset master data describes every security once.

46
00:03:53.886 --> 00:03:58.876
And regulatory reportings carry the templates for distributors and insurers.

47
00:03:59.528 --> 00:04:01.284
Every value has a clear name.

48
00:04:01.334 --> 00:04:06.640
Here is the fund's total net asset value, about two hundred seventy-nine million euro.

49
00:04:06.690 --> 00:04:10.190
A machine can process it, and a person can still read it.

50
00:04:11.340 --> 00:04:13.794
Not every delivery carries everything.

51
00:04:13.844 --> 00:04:18.538
A daily price feed carries prices and net asset values, and little else.

52
00:04:18.588 --> 00:04:21.502
A month-end delivery adds the full portfolio.

53
00:04:21.552 --> 00:04:25.653
A master data update carries new share classes or changed fees.

54
00:04:25.703 --> 00:04:31.253
And a regulatory delivery carries one or more templates for distributors or insurers.

55
00:04:31.303 --> 00:04:36.590
All of them use the same standard, so a receiver needs only one way to read them.

56
00:04:37.940 --> 00:04:41.711
Two more things matter in daily business: dates and corrections.

57
00:04:41.761 --> 00:04:47.860
Every delivery names its content date, the business date the data refers to, for example the month-end.

58
00:04:47.910 --> 00:04:54.128
And every price and every portfolio carries its own valuation date, so a file can even hold several days.

59
00:04:54.178 --> 00:04:58.069
When data was wrong, the sender does not silently send a new file.

60
00:04:58.119 --> 00:05:04.098
A correction is a delivery of its own, marked as a correction, and it names the delivery it replaces.

61
00:05:04.148 --> 00:05:09.053
So the receiver can follow every change, from the first delivery to the latest one.

62
00:05:10.403 --> 00:05:11.570
Who needs which part?

63
00:05:11.620 --> 00:05:17.768
Distributors and fund platforms need the share classes, their prices, their costs and their target markets.

64
00:05:17.818 --> 00:05:24.661
Insurers need the full portfolio with the master data of every position, so they can look through to the real holdings.

65
00:05:24.711 --> 00:05:29.469
Data vendors collect master data and prices from many fund houses and pass them on.

66
00:05:29.519 --> 00:05:35.841
And supervisors and central banks receive fund volumes, holdings and the national data their country asks for.

67
00:05:37.191 --> 00:05:40.329
That was what a FundsXML delivery contains.

68
00:05:40.379 --> 00:05:45.000
In the next film, you see how FundsXML carries regulatory data.

69
00:05:45.050 --> 00:05:46.483
Thanks for watching.
